---
/**
 * The full disclaimer. The two-line version lives in the footer of every page.
 *
 * NOT LEGAL ADVICE. Nothing here has been reviewed by a lawyer. The structure follows
 * the publisher's exclusion from the Investment Advisers Act as read in Lowe v. SEC,
 * 472 U.S. 181 (1985), which turns on three conditions: general and impersonal,
 * regular in cadence rather than timed to market events, and disinterested. The
 * footer sentence is the least important of the three. The impersonal condition is the
 * one that actually breaks, so it is stated on the page and on `/about/`.
 *
 * Securities counsel should review this page, and the whole site, before anything here
 * is monetised in any way — advertising, affiliate links, sponsorship, subscription or
 * paid consultation. Adding revenue changes which exclusion applies, and this wording
 * was not written for that case.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Disclaimer"
  description="General information about investing, published impersonally and on a schedule. Not advice, not a recommendation to buy or sell anything, and never an answer to an individual reader's question about their own portfolio."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>Disclaimer</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Disclaimer</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The short version">
      <p>
        The two lines in the footer of every page: general information about investing, and no part of it is
        personalised advice. This site does not answer questions about an individual reader's portfolio.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Everything on this site is general information about how investing works. None of it is advice, and none of
    it takes any account of your situation.</p>

    <p>Three sections below matter more than the rest. They are the conditions this site actually lives under, and
    breaking one of them would change what the site is.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>General</Rail>

    <h2 id="general">It is general, and it is impersonal</h2>

    <p>The research here was measured against reference cases: a US federal individual investor, particular tax
    brackets, particular account types, particular windows of history. You are not that person.</p>

    <p>Nothing on this site knows your age, your income, your tax bracket, your state, your debts, your job
    security, when you plan to stop working, what your employer's plan actually offers, or how you behaved the
    last time your account fell by a third. Every one of those changes the answer, and several of them change it
    more than anything this site has measured.</p>

    <p>None of it is a recommendation to buy or sell any security. Fund tickers appear here because somebody read
    the filings of the funds behind them; naming a fund is a measurement, never a suggestion that you own it. No
    offer and no solicitation of any kind is being made.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The condition</Rail>

    <h2 id="impersonal">This site never answers your question about your portfolio</h2>

    <p>Not by email. Not in an issue, not in a comment, not privately, not as a favour, not hypothetically, and
    not with the names changed.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Why this one is on the page">
      <p>
        The publisher's exclusion from the Investment Advisers Act holds as long as the writing stays "entirely
        impersonal". Reading someone's actual portfolio and telling them what to do with it is where it stops
        holding, and no footer text repairs that.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>An author who reads your holdings and tells you what to do with them has started giving personal investment
    advice, whatever the page says at the bottom. That is the condition that decides whether a publication is a
    publication, and it is a line rather than a spectrum.</p>

    <p>Questions about the method, an error in a number, a source that does not say what a page claims it says, or
    a piece of arithmetic you think is wrong: all welcome, and the answer will be a correction rather than a
    recommendation.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>
        If you want somebody to look at your own portfolio and tell you what to do, hire a fee-only fiduciary
        adviser who is paid by you and by nobody else. That is a different service from this one, and it is the
        right one for that question.
      </p>
    </Callout>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Cadence</Rail>

    <h2 id="cadence">It is published on a schedule, and never because the market moved</h2>

    <p>Pages here are revised when new evidence lands or on a date that was written into the page in advance. The
    trend page says it looks again at 48 filed months, around September 2027, and it will look then whether or not
    anything exciting has happened by then.</p>

    <p>Nothing on this site will ever be published in response to a market event. That is partly the legal
    condition and partly the project's own finding: a rule reviewed on price is indistinguishable from a rule
    abandoned in a drawdown, and this research has measured what that costs. Anchoring an annual rebalance in
    December, in June or in March moves the measured result by 0.19 percentage points a year, which is as large
    as the effect being measured. Reviewing on the calendar is the only review that means anything.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Risk</Rail>

    <h2 id="risk">Investing can lose you everything</h2>

    <p>All investing carries risk, including the total loss of the money you put in. Past performance tells you
    what happened; it does not tell you what will happen, and the difference between those two sentences is where
    most of the damage in this field gets done.</p>

    <p>Diversification reduces some risks and creates others. Every portfolio described here has spent long
    stretches behind a plain index fund, and the site says by how much: the lean toward cheap US companies has
    been behind by 54% over 17.7 years and has not caught up. Foreign stocks, 69% behind over 18.2 years. Those
    are the record rather than a simulated bad case.</p>

    <h3 id="backtests">Backtested results are hypothetical</h3>

    <p>A backtest is what a rule would have produced if somebody had run it, on data chosen after the fact, by
    someone who already knows how the story ends. It is hindsight with a chart attached. Nobody earned those
    returns, no fee was actually paid, no trade was actually filled, and no investor had to sit through the
    drawdown in the middle without knowing how it turned out.</p>

    <p>Every backtested number on this site should be read down for that, on top of the ranges and the search
    counts already printed beside it.</p>

    <h3 id="kelly">Position sizing assumes numbers nobody has</h3>

    <MarginNote label="Read every probability as a best case">
      <p>
        The arithmetic behind every probability here assumes the size of the advantage is known exactly. It is
        not. That assumption removes the largest source of uncertainty, so the real chance is lower than the
        printed one, and by an amount nobody can state.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>This site is named after the Kelly criterion, which computes the bet size that maximises long-run growth
    when you know the odds. The catch is the condition. You do not know the odds, nobody does, and the arithmetic
    does not degrade gently when you are wrong about them.</p>

    <p>Overestimate your advantage by a factor of two and the same formula that maximised growth now destroys it,
    while producing drawdowns most people cannot sit through on the way. Every position size on this site inherits
    that, and every probability derived from one is an upper bound rather than an estimate.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Scope</Rail>

    <h2 id="scope">Tax, jurisdiction and accuracy</h2>

    <p>The tax arithmetic here assumes a US federal individual investor at particular brackets, and it ignores
    every state and every local tax. It is not tax advice, and a result that holds in a US taxable account is
    sometimes exactly backwards elsewhere. Talk to somebody licensed before acting on any of it.</p>

    <p>No warranty is given on any of this: not on the accuracy of a number, not on a source still saying what it
    said when it was read, and not on the site being available. Data providers restate their own history without
    notice, and this project has been caught by that. The author accepts no liability for any loss arising from
    anything on this site, to the fullest extent the law allows.</p>

    <h3 id="uk">United Kingdom</h3>

    <p>This site is not directed at retail consumers in the United Kingdom, and nothing on it is a financial
    promotion under section 21 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000. The author is not authorised or
    regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority and gives no advice to anyone in the UK.</p>

    <h3 id="not-legal-advice">And this page is not legal advice either</h3>

    <p>It was written by the same non-lawyer who wrote the rest of the site, and it describes the conditions this
    project intends to keep rather than a legal opinion that it has kept them. Do not copy it for your own site.
    <a href="/about/">Who writes this</a> and <a href="/disclosures/">what it is paid for</a> are the two pages
    that put the rest of it in context.</p>

  </div>
</Base>
